      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "In recent studies, reduction in coronary flow velocity reserve (CFR) has been demonstrated in patients with increased aortic stiffness. Stress transoesophageal echocardiography (TEE) is a suitable method for the simultaneous evaluation of CFR and aortic stiffness parameters. The present study was designed to test whether increased echocardiography-derived aortic elastic modulus [E(p)] predicts impaired CFR in patients with suspected coronary artery disease (CAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The present study comprised 158 patients with suspected CAD. A CFR value < 2 was considered abnormal. Both men grade of aortic atherosclerosis (AA) (as a morphologic characteristic) (1.31 ± 0.68 vs. 1.02 ± 0.89, p < 0.05) and aortic distensibility (E(p) as a functional characteristic) (892 ± 594 mmHg vs. 723 ± 495 mmHg, P < 0.05) were increased in subjects with CFR < 2. In ROC analysis, the cut-off value for E(p) to predict impaired CFR was ≥ 670 mmHg, with 61% sensitivity and 61% specificity (ROC area 0.60, p = 0.026). The logistic regression model identified higher AA grade (hazard ratio (HR) 2.01, p < 0.05) and increased E(p) as independent predictors of reduced CFR (HE 1.10, p < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Increased aortic stiffness predicts impaired CFR in patients with suspected CAD."
        }
      ]},
